# The mutex contract: a free lock's invariant owns its cell's permission and
# the futex mutex hands it to one holder at a time. In the fork window an
# acquire the window serves (fork_holder_is_me) also takes a permission,
# one that stands for the fork handlers' hold of a lock prepare took (no
# arena, and so no lock, is built in the window), used by the forking
# thread alone until the release.
axiom_acquired	allocator invariant	none (assumption)
axiom_held	allocator invariant	none (assumption)
cell_ref	UnsafeCell/volatile/transmute	Verus
cell_mut	UnsafeCell/volatile/transmute	Verus
# loom (verus/WEAKMEM.md, P2): `LockGuard::drop`'s body is
# `RawLock::release` (`swap(UNLOCKED, Release)`, then a futex wake when it
# returned `CONTENDED`); the lock models run a copy of `RawLock` with a
# futex stand-in and check mutual exclusion, the acquire/release edge and
# no lost wake-up.
LockGuard::drop	atomics primitive	loom/GenMC	trusted (partly checked, loom: lock::loom_lock_two_threads and lock::loom_lock_three_threads; not checked: this body, which the models replace with a copy of `RawLock::release`, with two lockers and with three bounded at 5 preemptions; the kernel's futex, which the models replace with a stand-in that also orders the waker's accesses before the woken thread's return)
# `lock_slow`'s termination does not follow from the models: Drepper's
# mutex 3 is not starvation-free, so a woken waiter can lose the word to
# another thread's exchange as often as other threads retake the lock.
# The lock models show deadlock freedom (no lost wake-up between the
# `swap(CONTENDED)` and the sleep, nor for a sleeper behind another
# waiter) in their configurations (lock::loom_lock_two_threads, and
# lock::loom_lock_three_threads bounded at 5 preemptions, each locker
# taking the lock once, on a copy): evidence, not termination, which is
# all the contract claims, so the seam stays trusted.
RawLock::lock_slow	termination	loom/GenMC
# Lock-order premise of an entry point that holds no allocator lock
# (`Huge::usable_size`'s callers).
axiom_entry	allocator invariant	none (assumption)
# Lock-order premise inside `ClassState::decay_with`'s walk closure,
# which cannot carry the caller's token.
axiom_below_class	allocator invariant	none (assumption)
# Lock-order premise inside `Huge::decay_with`'s walk closure, which
# cannot carry the caller's token.
axiom_below_huge	allocator invariant	none (assumption)
# The fork prepare handler holds no lock at entry and keeps its token throughout.
axiom_fork_entry	allocator invariant	none (assumption)
# The fork window (design 9): the forking thread, recorded by
# `fork_window_open` at the end of the prepare handler and cleared by
# `fork_window_close` before the release, takes the held locks as
# acquired and releases nothing. Premise: it has no allocator critical
# section open in the window, so each lock's data is as its last holder
# left it and only that thread runs allocator code until the release.
# What the fork handlers snapshot or reset does not change in the window
# (the background thread's start is refused there: in_fork_window). Not
# covered: a process `_Fork`ed or cloned inside the window inherits the
# record as its own (design 9, residuals). No arena is built in the
# window (`Arenas::create` returns None, so `arenas.create` is EAGAIN;
# `Arenas::materialise` returns None, so an allocation whose home is not
# built is served from arena 0): every lock is one prepare took, the
# window serves only those, and the registry the release reads
# (`count_frozen`, `get_frozen`) is the one prepare locked.
# The window's record is verified code over two atomics (fork_holder_is_me,
# in_fork_window, fork_window_open, fork_window_close); the premise above
# rests on axiom_acquired, and the thread's identity on sys::pthread_self.
# The child's single thread before challoc's child handler: what
# `in_fork_child` stands for (no loom model covers the in-window reset).
in_child_fork_window	allocator invariant	none (assumption)
# A field projection: no `requires`, no `ensures`. The harness runs it on
# pointers to locks at four `T`s; the contract admits any pointer, and a
# projection through one that points into no lock is not defined in Rust.
Lock::value_ptr	raw pointer (internal)	Kani	trusted (partly checked, Kani: lock::kani_lock::value_ptr_is_the_value_field; not checked: a pointer that points into no live `Lock<T>`, which the contract admits and for which the projection is undefined (the one caller, `Arena::put_stats`, passes the pointer its permission names), and `T` other than `u8`, `u64`, `(u64, u8)` and `ArenaStats`)
